WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.360
Streamable HTTP is what happens when stdio falls short. It turns an MCP server into a network

2
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:14.159
accessible HTTP service using server sent events for streaming, and it supports both stateless

3
00:00:14.159 --> 00:00:19.920
and session-based state. Here's what you need to know to pick the right transport for any

4
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:26.479
deployment context. The moment a server has to be reachable from another machine, the transport has

5
00:00:26.479 --> 00:00:33.119
to become a real network service. That is streamable HTTP transport. It runs the MCP server

6
00:00:33.119 --> 00:00:39.360
as an HTTP service and supports both standard request response and streaming, and it's the

7
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:44.240
current standard for remote and production deployments. The streaming half comes from

8
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:51.360
server sent events, or SSE, a unidirectional HTTP protocol where the server pushes a sequence of

9
00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:58.000
events to the client over a long-lived connection. Then there's stateless HTTP, the request model

10
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.759
where each call carries all the context it needs, so the server stores nothing between calls.

11
00:01:03.759 --> 00:01:08.480
Streamable HTTP supports that model and a session-based one, and we'll look at both.

12
00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:16.720
Streamable HTTP works by turning the MCP server into a standard HTTP service. The server binds

13
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:23.839
to a port and exposes a single endpoint. To make a tool call, the client sends one HTTP post

14
00:01:23.839 --> 00:01:30.160
containing the MCP message. Here's the key detail. The server decides how to respond. It can reply

15
00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:36.559
with a plain JSON body, or it can upgrade that same response to a server sent events stream,

16
00:01:36.559 --> 00:01:42.639
pushing progress notifications first and then the final result. So the server initiates streaming,

17
00:01:42.639 --> 00:01:50.080
not the client. Separately, the client also opens a long-lived HTTP get connection, which gives the

18
00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:55.599
server a channel to push its own notifications and requests to the client. One endpoint, both

19
00:01:55.599 --> 00:02:02.319
directions covered. Server sent events is the streaming mechanism inside streamable HTTP,

20
00:02:02.319 --> 00:02:08.880
and it's worth understanding how it works at the protocol level. The client sends an HTTP request

21
00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:14.800
that stays open. Instead of sending a single response and closing the connection, the server

22
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:21.039
keeps writing event frames to the response stream as they become available. Each event frame follows

23
00:02:21.039 --> 00:02:28.080
the SSE text format, a data field containing the event payload, terminated by a blank line. The

24
00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:34.000
client reads these frames as they arrive. This is unidirectional. The server pushes, the client

25
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:41.839
receives. The client can't send messages over the same connection. For MCP, SSE is used to stream

26
00:02:41.839 --> 00:02:47.520
progress notifications back to the client while a long-running tool operation is still executing

27
00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:54.559
on the server. Streamable HTTP lets you choose between two state management approaches, and the

28
00:02:54.559 --> 00:03:02.639
choice has real scalability implications. Stateless HTTP means each request is completely self-contained.

29
00:03:02.639 --> 00:03:08.559
The client sends all the context it needs in the request body, and the server returns a result

30
00:03:08.559 --> 00:03:15.039
without storing anything between requests. Because no per-client state exists on the server, any

31
00:03:15.039 --> 00:03:21.839
server instance in a load-balanced pool can handle any request. This scales horizontally with no

32
00:03:21.839 --> 00:03:27.919
coordination overhead. Session-based state means the client sends a session token, and the server

33
00:03:27.919 --> 00:03:34.320
maintains a context store keyed to that token. This supports multi-turn interactions, where the

34
00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:40.320
server needs to remember what happened in earlier calls. It works, but it requires sticky sessions

35
00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:46.800
or a shared state backend to function correctly behind a load balancer. Three scenarios point

36
00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:53.600
clearly to streamable HTTP as the right choice. Remote clients. If the client and server are on

37
00:03:53.600 --> 00:04:00.240
different machines, you need an HTTP-based transport. There's no alternative because STDIO

38
00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:07.600
can't cross a network. Multi-user deployments. A single streamable HTTP server can handle

39
00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:13.279
concurrent connections from multiple clients. There's no need to spawn a dedicated process per

40
00:04:13.279 --> 00:04:21.200
user. Production APIs. HTTP gives you the entire production infrastructure stack. TLS for encryption,

41
00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:27.440
bearer tokens, or API keys for authentication, and load balancers for traffic distribution.

42
00:04:28.079 --> 00:04:33.760
These are things you'd have to build a completely separate layer for if you were using STDIO,

43
00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:39.440
and none of them would work remotely anyway. There's a naming point worth flagging here. In

44
00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:46.880
the older 2024-11-05 version of the MCP specification, there was a separate transport

45
00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:54.160
called HTTP plus SSE that has changed. Current MCP consolidates streaming under the streamable

46
00:04:54.160 --> 00:05:02.160
HTTP transport, and the old HTTP plus SSE transport is deprecated. So SSE today is the

47
00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:09.440
streaming delivery mechanism inside streamable HTTP, not a standalone peer to it. If you see

48
00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:16.480
the deprecated HTTP plus SSE name presented as a current separate option, the correct framing

49
00:05:16.559 --> 00:05:22.880
is that it's been folded into streamable HTTP. The transport that supports streaming in current

50
00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:31.679
MCP is streamable HTTP, full stop. When you deploy an MCP server over streamable HTTP in production,

51
00:05:31.679 --> 00:05:39.519
you take on standard HTTP service responsibilities that don't exist for STDIO. TLS is the first

52
00:05:39.519 --> 00:05:45.359
requirement. If the server is reachable over any untrusted network, all traffic should be encrypted.

53
00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:51.279
Authentication is the second. Every client request should carry a credential, either a

54
00:05:51.279 --> 00:05:58.320
bearer token or an API key, that the server validates before processing the MCP message.

55
00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:04.320
Load balancing is the third. A production server handling concurrent clients should be able to

56
00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:10.799
distribute load across multiple instances. All of this is standard infrastructure for any HTTP

57
00:06:10.799 --> 00:06:17.200
service, but it's a meaningful operational step up from the zero configuration model that STDIO

58
00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:24.399
provides. Let's make the transport selection decision concrete. Use STDIO when the server

59
00:06:24.399 --> 00:06:29.440
and client are on the same machine, when you're serving exactly one user at a time, and when you

60
00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:36.000
want zero network configuration. Local development tools, desktop applications that bundle their own

61
00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:43.839
MCP server, and cloud code integrations all fit this pattern. Use streamable HTTP when the client

62
00:06:43.839 --> 00:06:49.760
needs to reach the server over a network, when multiple users or multiple client applications

63
00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:57.279
need to share the same server, or when you need production infrastructure like TLS and authentication.

64
00:06:57.279 --> 00:07:02.320
The decision rule is simple. If the deployment crosses a network boundary or serves more than

65
00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:11.839
one user, STDIO is eliminated from consideration. People treat SSE and streamable HTTP as competing

66
00:07:11.839 --> 00:07:18.399
options on the same menu. They really aren't. Streamable HTTP is the transport, and server

67
00:07:18.399 --> 00:07:24.720
sent events is what the transport uses when a response needs to arrive in pieces rather than

68
00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:30.559
all at once. Get that relationship backwards, and you'll go hunting for a setting that was never

69
00:07:30.559 --> 00:07:36.239
really a transport to begin with. That's where we'll leave it on this one. I'll see you in the next lesson.

